{"step_index":25,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-19T19:17:02Z","content":"Research complete. Here's the bottom line:\n\n## The Short Answer\n\n**❌ You cannot merge RAM.** Connecting your 16GB MacBook to a 32GB Mac Mini will **never** give you 48GB. This is a **hard physics limitation** — Apple Silicon's memory is soldered millimeters from the CPU, running at 100-546 GB/s. Thunderbolt cables are **1,000× to 3,000× slower**. No hack, jailbreak, or black-hat method can overcome the laws of electromagnetic physics.\n\n**✅ But you CAN use the Mac Mini's full power** from your MacBook through these working methods:\n\n| Your Goal | Best Solution |\n|:---|:---|\n| Run heavy apps using Mac Mini's power | **Screen Sharing** over Thunderbolt cable |\n| Code on MacBook, compile on Mac Mini | **VS Code Remote SSH** |\n| Run AI models too big for 16GB | **Exo** — pools both Macs' memory for LLMs |\n| Faster video exports | **Apple Compressor** distributed processing |\n\nThe **coolest hacker method** is **Exo** — it lets your 16GB MacBook + 32GB Mac Mini team up to run AI models that neither could handle alone, splitting model layers across both machines' GPUs.\n\nThe full report with all technical details, bandwidth comparisons, architecture diagrams, and every method (official + hacker + dangerous) is in the artifact above. 👆"}
